 -the-forms-of-world-weary-wisdom\n\nThe Research Workshop in Literature\, 
 Philosophy\, and the Arts is proud to host a discussion with Karen Zumhage
 n-Yekplé (Tulane University) on I Bet That's Also a Platitude: Kafka\, Th
 e Coen Brother\, and the Forms of  World-Weary Wisdom.\n\nA discussion wil
 l be had on two pieces that explore what wisdom sounds like once the world
  is too worn for it. “The Everyday’s Fabulous Beyond” (chapter 2 of 
 A Different Order of Difficulty\, 2020) reads Kafka’s “On Parables” 
 alongside the Tractatus as puzzle texts of the same moment: each built on 
 the gap between daily life\, “which is all we have\,” and a fabulous B
 eyond it cannot name\; each teaching by nonsense and likeness rather than 
 by anything it says\; each ending with no one having the last word. “It 
 Is What It Is: Wisdom’s World-Weary Forms” (forthcoming in The Cambrid
 ge Companion to Literature and Philosophy\, 2027\; circulated here as the 
 seed of a longer essay) follows that argument into the Coen brothers’ A 
 Serious Man\, where a Job-like physics professor goes looking for guidance
  in perplexity and receives\, from three rabbis\, Rashi\, and Jefferson Ai
 rplane\, the forms a weary world leaves available: tautology\, nonsense\, 
 questions that are questions only in outward shape\, parable\, and platitu
 de. The workshop question is the one Kafka’s last line raises and the Co
 en chapter’s last line repeats: whether the parable and the platitude ar
 e opposites or neighbors\, and what\, if anything\, separates “receive w
 ith simplicity everything that happens
